@chapter 7 | Waves and Sound

@intro
This chapter has two parts. **Electromagnetic waves**: the order of the spectrum by frequency, radio waves and the ionosphere, microwaves for communication, why dish TV fails in rain and why TV signals have a limited range. **Sound**: its need for a medium and its speed in solids, liquids and gases, echo and reverberation, **ultrasound** (bats, cleaning, medicine), noise limits in decibels, and the remote control and FM sound of television. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 7.1 | The Electromagnetic Spectrum

@src 7:1
@pyq U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Pre) 2023
Consider the following and arrange these in increasing order of frequency :
I. X-rays
II. Visible rays
III. Infrared rays
IV. Radio waves
Select the correct answer using the code given below :
Code :
(a) III, II, I, IV
(b) I, II, III, IV
(c) II, III, IV, I
(d) IV, III, II, I
@ans (d)
@end

@src 7:2
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2025
Arrange the following electromagnetic radiations in decreasing order of their frequencies:
1. Gamma rays
2. Ultraviolet rays
3. Radio waves
4. X-rays
Choose the correct answer from the code given below:
Code:
(a) 1, 4, 3, 2
(b) 4, 1, 2, 3
(c) 1, 4, 2, 3
(d) 4, 1, 3, 2
@ans (c)
@end

@table
Radiation (rising frequency, falling wavelength) | Typical wavelength | Uses
**Radio waves** | Metres to km | Radio, TV broadcast, mobile phones (UHF)
**Microwaves** | mm to 30 cm | **Satellite and mobile communication**, radar, microwave ovens, Wi-Fi
**Infrared** | 780 nm - 1 mm | **TV remote**, night vision, heat lamps, thermal imaging
**Visible light** | 390-780 nm | Sight, optical fibre
**Ultraviolet** | 10-390 nm | Sterilising water, forming vitamin D, detecting forged notes
**X-rays** | 0.01-10 nm | Radiography, **CT scan**, airport baggage scanners
**Gamma rays** | Below 0.01 nm | Cancer therapy, sterilising medical equipment, food irradiation
@end

All electromagnetic waves travel at the speed of light in vacuum; they differ only in frequency and wavelength. Energy rises with frequency, so **gamma rays** are the most energetic and penetrating.

@q 7:3

@src1 7:1
@one
Wireless signals are reflected back to the Earth's surface by the
@ans ionosphere
@end

The **ionosphere** (about 80-600 km) holds charged layers that reflect **medium and short radio waves**, which lets short-wave radio reach other continents. TV and FM signals (VHF/UHF) pass through it, so they work only up to the **horizon**: the Earth's curvature limits range, which is why TV towers are tall and satellites are used for long distances.

@q 7:4-5

The Sun's **short-wave** radiation passes through the air and heats the ground; the ground then radiates **long-wave (infrared) terrestrial radiation**, which the air, water vapour and CO_{2} absorb. So the atmosphere is heated mainly **from below**. **Cosmic rays** are not electromagnetic waves but high-energy **charged particles** (mostly protons) from the Sun and distant supernovae.

@q 7:6-9

Satellite TV uses microwaves of a few centimetres. **Heavy rain** both **absorbs** and **scatters** them ("rain fade"), and a small dish has little margin to compensate, so all three statements are accepted. A **CT (computed tomography) scan** builds cross-sections from many **X-ray** images; MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves, and ultrasonography uses sound.

@section 7.2 | Propagation and Speed of Sound

@q 8:1-4

@src1 8:2
@one
An astronaut cannot hear his companion on the Moon's surface because
@ans there is no medium for the transmission of sound.
@end

@table
Medium | Speed of sound (approx.)
Vacuum | Zero: sound cannot travel
Air (0°C) | 332 m/s (about 343 m/s at 20°C)
Water | About 1,480 m/s
**Steel** | About 5,000-6,000 m/s
@end

Sound is a **mechanical, longitudinal** wave that needs a medium, so it travels **fastest in solids**, slower in liquids and slowest in gases (**V_{air} < V_{water} < V_{steel}**). Its speed in air **rises with temperature** and humidity but does not depend on pressure. Astronauts on the airless Moon talk by radio.

@q 8:5

**Pitch** depends on **frequency**. Men's vocal cords are usually **longer and thicker** and vibrate more slowly, so their voices are deeper; women's and children's voices have higher pitch. **Loudness** depends on amplitude and **quality (timbre)** on the overtones present.

@section 7.3 | Echo, Reverberation and Ultrasound

@q 8:6

@src 8:7
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2007
The walls of the hall, built for music concerts should :
(a) Amplify sound
(b) Transmit sound
(c) Reflect sound
(d) Absorb sound
@ans (d)
@end

@src 8:8
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2007
To hear a clear echo, the minimum distance between the reflecting surface and the observer should be :
(a) 165 feet
(b) 165 metre
(c) 16.5 feet
(d) 16.5 metre
@ans (d)
@end

The ear keeps a sound for about **1/10 second**, so an echo is heard separately only if it returns at least 0.1 s later. In 0.1 s sound travels about 33-34 m, there and back, so the wall must be at least **about 17 m** away (16.5 m at 0°C). **Reverberation** is the persistence of sound in a hall from repeated reflections; concert halls use **sound-absorbing** curtains, carpets, panels and false ceilings to control it.

@src 8:9
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2004
Consider the following statements about ultrasonic waves :
1. They can destroy insects.
2. They can clean clothes by removing dust.
3. They can be used to treat diseases.
4. They can control automatic doors.
Of the above statements
(a) 1 and 2 are correct
(b) 3 and 4 are correct
(c) 1, 2 and 3 are correct
(d) All are correct
@ans (d)
@end

@q 8:10-12

@table
Sound | Frequency | Examples
**Infrasonic** | Below 20 Hz | Earthquakes, heartbeat, elephants and whales communicating
**Audible** | 20 Hz - 20,000 Hz | Human hearing
**Ultrasonic** | Above 20,000 Hz | **Bats** (echolocation), dolphins, dog whistles, **SONAR**, ultrasonography, kidney-stone breaking, ultrasonic cleaning, detecting flaws in metals
@end

**Mach number** describes the speed of a body compared with sound, not the speed of sound itself; statement 2 is also false because sound is fastest in solids. The source's key (c) treats statement 3 as correct, reading it loosely as "used to express speeds in terms of sound".

@section 7.4 | Noise Limits

@q 8:13

@src 8:14
@pyq U.P. P.C.S. (Pre) 2022
In the context of permissible noise levels match List-I with List-II and select the answer from the code given below the lists.
@match
List-I (Area) | List-II (Permissible Noise Level)
A. Residential area | 1. 50 dB
B. Silent zone | 2. 55 dB
C. Industrial area | 3. 65 dB
D. Commercial area | 4. 70 dB
@endmatch
Code : A B C D
(a) 3 4 2 1
(b) 1 2 3 4
(c) 2 1 3 4
(d) 2 1 4 3
@ans (d)
@end

@table
Area (Noise Pollution Rules, 2000) | Day limit dB(A) | Night limit dB(A)
Industrial | 75 | 70
Commercial | 65 | 55
Residential | 55 | 45
**Silence zone** (100 m around hospitals, schools, courts) | 50 | 40
@end

The rules set the industrial day limit at 75 dB; the question's 70 dB is the nearest option. Night is 10 pm to 6 am. **Loudspeakers** need permission and are banned at night except in a few notified cases.

@q 8:15

The **decibel** scale is logarithmic: every **10 dB** means ten times the intensity. A whisper is about 30 dB, conversation 60 dB, heavy traffic 80-90 dB and a jet take-off 120-140 dB (pain threshold about 120 dB). Long exposure above **85 dB** can cause permanent hearing loss.

@section 7.5 | Musical Instruments, Remote Controls and Television

@q 8:16

A plucked sitar or guitar string vibrates in **stationary (standing), transverse** waves; the air column of a flute vibrates in stationary **longitudinal** waves.

@q 8:17-18

@qnote 8:17 | The source keys **radio waves**, but most TV remotes use **infrared** light (an IR LED), as the next question shows. Some newer set-top-box and smart-TV remotes use radio (Bluetooth or RF). If both appear, choose infrared.

@q 8:19-21

Analogue TV sent **picture by amplitude modulation (AM)** and **sound by frequency modulation (FM)**, which resists noise. India's terrestrial TV (Doordarshan) has since shifted largely to digital (DVB-T2) and free-dish satellite broadcasting.

@trap
**Radio < microwave < infrared < visible < UV < X-ray < gamma** in frequency. **Cosmic rays are particles**, not EM waves. The atmosphere is heated by **long-wave terrestrial** radiation. Sound is fastest in **steel**, and cannot travel in vacuum. Echo needs **about 17 m**. Ultrasound is **above 20 kHz**. TV remotes use **infrared**. Silence zone limit = **50 dB** by day.
@end

